   <dl class='publication-summary'> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Problem</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>Self-supervised objectives such as VICReg learn representations by
making related inputs similar, while variance and covariance penalties prevent
collapse. These penalties are statistics over a batch of samples, so layer-local
variants, too, are typically local in space but not in time.</dd></div> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Idea</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>We introduce
Temporal Regularized Learning (TeReL), which optimizes a VICReg-style
objective one sample at a time with purely local updates: each update uses only
the current activity of its own and the preceding layer and a fixed-size running
state.</dd></div> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Mechanism</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>Temporally adjacent inputs serve as positive pairs,
which makes the objective a soft form of Slow Feature Analysis (SFA).
Running estimates of each neuron’s mean and variance, together with one
lateral matrix per layer, replace the batch statistics; held fixed during
differentiation, they turn the gradient into a Hebbian feedforward update and an
anti-Hebbian lateral update.</dd></div> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Evidence</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>We show that a two-layer network
trained with batch size one on an MNIST stream of class-homogeneous
chunks reaches 96.48% linear-probe accuracy, above random features
(95.09%) and classical SFA, and that the temporal order and each loss term
contribute to this result. With both locality constraints relaxed, the same
objective matches supervised backpropagation.</dd></div> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Significance</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>TeReL thus
shows that the collapse-preventing statistics of modern self-supervised
learning can be held as running state at each neuron, which brings these
objectives closer to what brains and neuromorphic hardware can implement.</dd></div>
</dl>
